<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">People's Republic of Korea</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – 1946 CE · capital Seoul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A government proclaimed by Korean leaders in Seoul in September 1945, after liberation from Japan, and rejected by the American occupation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> surrender the moderate leftist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyuh-woon-hyung">Lyuh Woon-hyung</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-for-the-preparation-of-korean-independence">Committee for the Preparation of Korean Independence</a>, which organised local committees to keep order. On 6 September 1945, two days before American troops landed, a national assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> proclaimed the People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its cabinet named prominent figures, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a> as chairman, often without their consent, and leftists dominated. People's committees across the country ran local affairs, organised land and labour programmes and took over Japanese property in many areas.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-army-military-government-in-korea">United States Army Military Government in Korea</a> refused to recognise it, declared in October 1945 that it had no authority and later suppressed its committees in the south; it faded by early 1946. In the north, its committees were absorbed into the Soviet-backed administration.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peoples-republic-of-korea.jpg" alt="People's Republic of Korea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_People%27s_Committee_of_Korea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seoul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lyuh Woon-hyung, Heo Heon</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>PRK, Joseon Inmin Gonghwaguk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1946&amp;focus=peoples-republic-of-korea" aria-label="People's Republic of Korea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7853 826 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where People's Republic of Korea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8173 1082L8172 1082M8173 1082L8173 1082"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8172 1082h0M8173 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8173 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8173 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Seoul. <a href="/atlas?year=1946&amp;focus=peoples-republic-of-korea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
